    <!-- 09 CLOUD / WHERE IT RUNS -->
    <section class="panel" id="cloud" data-rung="cloud">
      <div class="panel-inner reveal">
        <div class="eyebrow"><span class="rung-tag">12 · Shipping it</span> Where it runs — the cloud</div>
        <h2>A great agent still needs somewhere to live.</h2>
        <p class="lede">"The cloud" is just someone else's computers, rented by the minute. The real question is never <em>which is best</em> — it's <strong>which fits this job.</strong> There are three tiers, and the choice is usually made for you by one thing: <span class="accent-ocean">where your data is allowed to be.</span></p>

        <div class="arch-grid">
          <div class="arch">
            <div class="ak" style="color:var(--ocean-soft)">Tier 1 · Hyperscalers</div>
            <h3>Azure · AWS · GCP</h3>
            <p>Full control, every service, enterprise compliance and data residency. Heavy to set up. <b style="color:var(--text)">Reach for it when:</b> regulated data, an existing enterprise contract, or real scale.</p>
            <div class="built">why each → <b style="color:var(--ocean-soft)">Azure</b> if the org is Microsoft (AD, 365, Azure OpenAI keeps the model in-tenant) · <b style="color:var(--ocean-soft)">AWS</b> for breadth · <b style="color:var(--ocean-soft)">GCP</b> for data/ML</div>
          </div>
          <div class="arch">
            <div class="ak" style="color:var(--green)">Tier 2 · App platforms</div>
            <h3>Vercel · Render · Supabase · Fly</h3>
            <p>Managed, cheap to start, live in minutes — no VPC to provision. You trade control for speed. <b style="color:var(--text)">Reach for it when:</b> a startup, a prototype, or you need to ship <em>this week.</em></p>
            <div class="built">this is where my own agents run → <b>Render + Supabase + Vercel + Stripe</b></div>
          </div>
          <div class="arch">
            <div class="ak" style="color:var(--amber)">Tier 3 · The AI layer</div>
            <h3>Model + inference hosts</h3>
            <p>The model itself is usually an API — OpenAI, Anthropic, or Azure OpenAI. Custom or open models run on Modal / Replicate; Streamlit for quick demos. <b style="color:var(--text)">Reach for it when:</b> always — it sits on top of tier 1 or 2.</p>
            <div class="built">decision → managed API by default; self-host only for cost, latency, or data control</div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="biz">
          <div class="proj">Why I've used which · PwC (Azure) vs my own stack (Render)</div>
          <p>At PwC I shipped on <span class="val">Azure</span> because banking clients required data residency and already had enterprise agreements — Azure OpenAI meant the model ran <em>inside their tenant</em>, which is often the whole reason a regulated org picks a cloud at all. For my own products I run <span class="val">Render + Supabase + Vercel</span> — because I need to ship this week, not provision infrastructure. Same engineer, opposite choice, and I can explain the trade either direction.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 10 RELIABILITY -->
    <section class="panel" id="reliability" data-rung="reliability">
      <div class="panel-inner reveal">
        <div class="eyebrow"><span class="rung-tag">13 · The hard part</span> Evals + LangSmith</div>
        <h2>Anyone can demo an agent once. I ship the 1,000th run.</h2>
        <p class="lede">The value isn't the demo — it's the run on the input nobody predicted, without burning money or going off the rails. Two disciplines: <strong class="accent-green">evaluation</strong> (measure whether it meets the bar) and <strong class="accent-ocean">monitoring</strong> (watch whether it still does in production, with a full trace of every step).</p>

        <div class="trace" aria-label="Example LangSmith-style trace">
          <div class="trace-head"><span>trace · file_appeal run #8f2a</span><span class="tot">1,240 tokens · 4.2s · <span style="color:var(--green)">passed</span></span></div>
          <div class="tnode"><span class="tw llm"></span><span class="tlabel">agent.reason <span class="tk">"denial → find criteria"</span></span><span class="tms">0.6s</span><span class="ttok">180 tok</span></div>
          <div class="tindent">
            <div class="tnode"><span class="tw tool"></span><span class="tlabel">tool · retrieve_regulation <span class="tk">(CMS, MRI lumbar)</span></span><span class="tms">1.1s</span><span class="ttok">—</span></div>
            <div class="tnode"><span class="tw ret"></span><span class="tlabel">retrieved <span class="tk">§220.2 · 3 chunks · reranked</span></span><span class="tms">0.3s</span><span class="ttok">420 tok</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="tnode"><span class="tw llm"></span><span class="tlabel">agent.reason <span class="tk">"criteria met → draft appeal"</span></span><span class="tms">0.9s</span><span class="ttok">240 tok</span></div>
          <div class="tindent">
            <div class="tnode"><span class="tw tool"></span><span class="tlabel">tool · file_appeal <span class="tk">(cites=[§220.2])</span></span><span class="tms">1.3s</span><span class="ttok">200 tok</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="tnode"><span class="tw ret"></span><span class="tlabel">eval · <span style="color:var(--green)">grounded?</span> yes &nbsp; <span style="color:var(--green)">cited?</span> yes &nbsp; <span style="color:var(--green)">policy-safe?</span> yes</span><span class="tms">judge</span><span class="ttok">—</span></div>
          <div class="trace-legend">
            <span><i style="background:var(--ocean)"></i>LLM step</span><span><i style="background:var(--amber)"></i>tool call</span>
            <span><i style="background:var(--green)"></i>retrieval / eval</span><span><i style="background:var(--coral)"></i>failure</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="beats" style="margin-top:24px">
          <div class="card">
            <div class="card-label"><span class="dot green"></span>Evals — does it meet the bar?</div>
            <p>A golden set + assertions, run on <em>every</em> change so "improving" one case can't silently break another. Offline, measured, repeatable. <b class="accent-green">My LangGraph Eval Harness.</b></p>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <div class="card-label"><span class="dot ocean"></span>Monitoring — is it still?</div>
            <p>A wrong answer still returns 200 OK, so I watch three layers: <b>operational</b> (latency, cost/run), <b>quality</b> (judge scores, thumbs, task success), and the <b>trace</b> above — the only way to answer "why did it do that."</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="beats" style="margin-top:18px">
          <div class="card">
            <div class="card-label"><span class="dot coral"></span>Capability ≠ reliability</div>
            <p><b>pass@1</b> says it <em>can</em> work; production needs <b>pass^k</b> — correct on <em>all</em> k trials, because LLM routing is stochastic and one green run hides flakiness. Add paraphrase perturbations and injected tool failures and you have reliability's three axes.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <div class="card-label"><span class="dot green"></span>My own numbers (Meridian, k=3, live)</div>
            <p>Originals <b>100% pass^k</b> · paraphrases <b>88%</b> · provider-outage fallback floor <b>15/15</b> · cross-family judge (GPT grading Claude) <b>4/5 faithful</b>. Best part: the harness <b>found two real bugs</b> — a paraphrase my router misroutes and an unsupported claim my writer slipped in. That's the eval earning its keep.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="biz">
          <div class="proj">The loop that closes it</div>
          <p>A bad production trace becomes a golden test — so that failure can never quietly return. <span class="val">Monitoring feeds evals feeds the next change.</span> That's the whole discipline, and where most "AI demos" fall apart.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 11 ENERGY WALL -->
    <section class="panel" id="energy" data-rung="energy">
      <div class="panel-inner reveal">
        <div class="eyebrow" style="color:var(--coral)"><span class="rung-tag" style="color:var(--coral);opacity:.7">Part II · The frontier</span> The energy wall</div>
        <h2>Everything above works. The problem is what it costs to <em>run.</em></h2>
        <p class="lede">The quiet fact under the whole AI boom: it's staggeringly <strong>energy-hungry</strong>. Training a frontier model draws megawatts for weeks; then serving it to millions is a permanent, larger draw. Data centers are already a meaningful slice of global electricity — and the water to cool them is the constraint nobody tweets about.</p>

        <div class="anim-stage" data-anim="energy" style="margin-top:24px">
          <div style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:0.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--text-mute);margin-bottom:16px">roughly what it takes to do "intelligence"</div>
          <div id="energyBars" style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:12px;max-width:560px"></div>
          <div class="seeing" style="margin-top:20px"><b>The gut-punch:</b> your brain does everything it does on about <b>20 watts</b> — less than a light bulb. The data center answering your chatbot question runs on a scale millions of times larger. Nature already solved efficient intelligence; silicon hasn't.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="beats" style="margin-top:26px">
          <div class="card">
            <div class="card-label"><span class="dot coral"></span>Why we can't just wait for faster chips</div>
            <p>For 40 years chips got faster almost for free — <b style="color:var(--text)">Moore's law</b>. That's slowing. Transistors are now a <em>few atoms</em> wide; shrink them more and electrons leak straight through as wasted heat. <b style="color:var(--text)">Dennard scaling</b> — power dropping as transistors shrank — already ended around 2005. The free lunch is over.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <div class="card-label"><span class="dot coral"></span>So the bottleneck moved</div>
            <p>We stopped getting speed from smaller transistors and started getting it from <em>more</em> of them — more GPUs, more power, more heat. That's a spending race, not a physics win. The real question becomes: <b style="color:var(--text)">what if we computed a fundamentally different way?</b></p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 12 BEYOND SILICON -->
    <section class="panel" id="beyond" data-rung="beyond">
      <div class="panel-inner reveal">
        <div class="eyebrow" style="color:var(--coral)"><span class="rung-tag" style="color:var(--coral);opacity:.7">Part II · The frontier</span> Beyond silicon</div>
        <h2>If shrinking transistors is done, the next leap changes the <em>architecture</em> — or the physics itself.</h2>
        <p class="lede">People lump these together; they're really two different escapes. Some <strong>rearrange the chips</strong> — still silicon, radically different design. Others <strong>change the medium</strong> — compute with light, molecules, or quantum states. And one thing here isn't hardware at all. <span class="accent-coral">Honest labels below; the numbers are lab / vendor benchmarks on favorable workloads, not guarantees.</span></p>

        <div class="arch-grid">
          <div class="arch">
            <div class="ak" style="color:var(--ocean-soft)">New architecture · still silicon</div>
            <h3>Neuromorphic — compute like a brain</h3>
            <p>Chips of <em>spiking</em> neurons that fire only on a signal, with memory and compute fused so data never shuttles back and forth. Intel Loihi 2, IBM NorthPole, SpiNNaker.</p>
            <div class="built">the bet → up to ~100× efficiency on sparse, event-driven work · the catch → <b>not</b> transformers, and the software's immature</div>
          </div>
          <div class="arch">
            <div class="ak" style="color:var(--ocean-soft)">New architecture · still silicon</div>
            <h3>Analog in-memory — math inside the memory</h3>
            <p>Memristors do the matrix-multiply <em>where the data already lives</em> — killing the shuttle between memory and processor that burns most of the energy in a GPU.</p>
            <div class="built">the bet → order-of-magnitude efficiency (memristor compute-in-memory) · the catch → analog noise vs accuracy</div>
          </div>
          <div class="arch">
            <div class="ak" style="color:var(--green)">New medium · light</div>
            <h3>Photonic — compute with light</h3>
            <p>Matrix multiplies happen as <em>light interfering</em> through a mesh — and light also moves data between chips far cheaper than copper. Lightmatter, Lightelligence.</p>
            <div class="built">the bet → ~10–100× less energy per operation · the catch → precision &amp; integration, still needs electronics around it</div>
          </div>
          <div class="arch">
            <div class="ak" style="color:var(--coral)">New medium · qubits</div>
            <h3>Quantum — a different question entirely</h3>
            <p>The honest take, against the headlines: <em>not</em> faster AI and <em>not</em> an energy fix. It's a specialized co-processor for simulation, optimization, and sampling — used <em>alongside</em> classical, not instead of it.</p>
            <div class="built">the reality → no clear speedup for mainstream deep learning · error correction still unsolved</div>
          </div>
          <div class="arch">
            <div class="ak" style="color:var(--amber)">New medium · the wild frontier</div>
            <h3>Chemical &amp; molecular</h3>
            <p>Compute in the physics itself — reaction-diffusion waves (Belousov–Zhabotinsky), DNA and molecular logic. Massively parallel, tiny energy, deeply strange.</p>
            <div class="built">the bet → nature computes in parallel for free · the catch → almost impossible to program or read in real time</div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="biz" style="border-left-color:var(--violet)">
          <div class="proj" style="color:var(--violet)">Where I actually work — and why it's <em>not</em> a chip above</div>
          <p>Hyperdimensional computing isn't a substrate — it's a <span class="val">representation</span>: encode meaning as huge vectors, and it runs beautifully on exactly the neuromorphic and in-memory hardware above. That's the layer I research — presented at the Science of Consciousness Conference.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 13 OPEN QUESTIONS -->
    <!-- 16 FRONTIER READING LIST -->
    <section class="panel" id="frontier" data-rung="frontier">
      <div class="panel-inner reveal">
        <div class="eyebrow" style="color:var(--coral)"><span class="rung-tag" style="color:var(--coral);opacity:.7">Part II · The frontier</span> The reading list — upskilling in public</div>
        <h2>The papers I'm actually reading, and the one-line take on each.</h2>
        <p class="lede">The winters section taught me to distrust vibes — including my own. This is my running reading log: what's genuinely state-of-the-art in making agents <em>reliable</em>, and where the field is arguing. <span class="accent-ocean">Updated as I go — last pass Aug 2026.</span></p>
        <div class="beats" style="margin-top:22px">
          <div class="card">
            <div class="card-label"><span class="dot green"></span>Reliability science</div>
            <p><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.06112" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ReliabilityBench</a> — reliability = consistency (pass^k) + robustness to paraphrase + fault tolerance; the framework my eval_v2 implements. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29231" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Beyond pass@1</a> — why single-run benchmarks systematically overstate production readiness.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <div class="card-label"><span class="dot ocean"></span>Context &amp; memory</div>
            <p><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.10209" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Less Context, Better Agents</a> — recency window + running summary wins; changed my defaults. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09023" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Demand Paging for Context Windows</a> — context as RAM, store as disk: the memory-hierarchy frame.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <div class="card-label"><span class="dot ocean"></span>Safe tool use</div>
            <p><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08012" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Verifiably Safe Tool Use</a> — capability/trust labels on tools, guarantees over vibes. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.19449" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Guardrails as Scapegoats</a> — model-side refusals are unfaithful; deterministic gates aren't. Academic backing for "the model proposes, code commits."</p>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <div class="card-label"><span class="dot amber"></span>Judging the judges</div>
            <p><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.23178" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bias mitigation in LLM-as-judge pipelines</a> — never judge with the generator's own family, pairwise both orderings, explicit rubrics, calibrate on your own labels. My faithfulness judge follows all four.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <div class="card-label"><span class="dot coral"></span>The retrieval argument: is RAG enough? Do we need graphs?</div>
            <p><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.09666" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Do We Still Need GraphRAG?</a> — agentic (multi-round) search substantially closes the gap to GraphRAG on plain vector RAG; graphs still win deep multi-hop once their index cost is amortized. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.25656" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Is GraphRAG Needed?</a> continues the argument with context optimization. Names to know: <b>HippoRAG2</b> (PageRank over an entity graph), <b>LightRAG</b> (dual-level graph + vectors), <b>LinearRAG</b> (relation-free, linear cost). My take: <span class="val">run the baseline and publish the table — "graphs weren't worth it here, and here's the number" beats a fancy demo.</span></p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="biz">
          <div class="proj">Why this section exists</div>
          <p>Reading lists rot in bookmarks. Publishing mine forces the one-line take — and the take is the actual skill: <span class="val">knowing what each result changes about how you build tomorrow.</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="panel" id="questions" data-rung="questions">
      <div class="panel-inner reveal">
        <div class="eyebrow" style="color:var(--violet)"><span class="rung-tag" style="color:var(--violet);opacity:.7">Part III · The big questions</span> Where the engineering runs out</div>
        <h2>The questions I don't get to answer at work — but can't stop thinking about.</h2>
        <p class="lede">Past a certain point the build stops and the <em>wondering</em> starts. Three questions sit at that edge for me. I keep them <strong>scientific on purpose</strong> — these are where I might actually contribute; the policy fights I leave to people who enjoy them.</p>

        <div class="arch-grid">
          <div class="arch" style="border-color:rgba(180,140,240,0.35)">
            <div class="ak" style="color:var(--violet)">Consciousness &amp; the medium</div>
            <h3>If a chip runs the same computation as a brain, is anyone home?</h3>
            <p>Does mind depend on what it's <em>made of</em>? <b style="color:var(--text)">Functionalism</b> says no — the computation is all that matters, so a photonic or neuromorphic mind would count. <b style="color:var(--text)">Biological naturalism</b> says the wetware matters. <b style="color:var(--text)">Integrated Information Theory</b> tries to put a number on it. The uncomfortable version — Chalmers' <em>hard problem</em> — is that you could explain every last computation and still not know why it <em>feels</em> like anything from the inside.</p>
            <div class="built">why it's mine → my HDC work, presented at the <b>Science of Consciousness Conference</b></div>
          </div>
          <div class="arch">
            <div class="ak" style="color:var(--coral)">AI alignment</div>
            <h3>Getting a system to want what you <em>meant.</em></h3>
            <p>The scientific version, not the sci-fi one: how do you make a capable system pursue the goal you intended, not the one you literally wrote? <b style="color:var(--text)">Specification gaming</b>, reward hacking, scalable oversight — a model smart enough to satisfy the <em>letter</em> of your metric and miss the point. It's my eval work scaled up: <span style="color:var(--text)">evaluation is alignment at the frontier.</span></p>
            <div class="built">my take → <span style="color:var(--text-mute)">essay in progress — drops here</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="arch">
            <div class="ak" style="color:var(--green)">Interpretability</div>
            <h3>Right answer, black box — not good enough.</h3>
            <p>A model can be correct and still unreadable, and in healthcare or finance "trust me" isn't an answer. <b style="color:var(--text)">Explainability</b> makes a decision auditable — <em>why</em> was this claim denied? <b style="color:var(--text)">Mechanistic interpretability</b> is the deeper science of reading what the weights actually do. My Prior Auth agent citing the exact reg is the shipping version; reading the circuits is the research version.</p>
            <div class="built">already in my work → the "with receipts" in every agent I build</div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="seeing" style="margin-top:24px;border-color:rgba(180,140,240,0.3)"><b style="color:var(--violet)">On ethics, deliberately:</b> I keep this page to the technical questions I can move — alignment and interpretability are the engineering face of "AI ethics." The political and policy fights are real, but they're not what I'd put on an engineer's résumé, and pretending otherwise reads as posturing. Focus is a signal too.</div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- CLOSE -->
    <section class="panel" id="close" data-rung="questions">
      <div class="panel-inner">
        <div class="eyebrow" style="justify-content:center">The takeaway</div>
        <h2>From "what's a token" to the questions nobody's answered yet.</h2>
        <p class="lede" style="margin-left:auto;margin-right:auto;text-align:center">That's the whole arc — the basics, how they work, what I build on top, where computing itself is headed, and the questions I chase past the edge of it. Point at anything here and I can go a level deeper, in concept or in code.</p>
        <div class="close-links">
          <a class="btn primary" href="/RESUME_2026.pdf">Download résumé</a>
          <a class="btn" href="mailto:austinnafe@aol.com">austinnafe@aol.com</a>
          <a class="btn" href="https://github.com/anafe03">GitHub</a>
          <a class="btn" href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/austinnafe/">LinkedIn</a>
        </div>
        <p style="text-align:center;margin-top:40px;font-family:var(--mono);font-size:12.5px;color:var(--text-mute);max-width:58ch;margin-left:auto;margin-right:auto;line-height:1.75">🤖 Generated with AI, this one — I'll own it. The day one of these is my own typing, you won't need the disclaimer to tell. Fewer em-dashes, more typos. Trust me, you'll know.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</div>

<script is:inline>
(function () {
  var reduce = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;

  // reveal
  var reveals = Array.prototype.slice.call(document.querySelectorAll('.reveal'));
  var ro = new IntersectionObserver(function (es) {
    es.forEach(function (e) { if (e.isIntersecting) { e.target.classList.add('in'); ro.unobserve(e.target); } });
  }, { threshold: 0.1 });
  reveals.forEach(function (r) { ro.observe(r); });
  function revealNear() { reveals.forEach(function (r) { var b = r.getBoundingClientRect(); if (b.top < innerHeight * 1.1 && b.bottom > -50) { r.classList.add('in'); ro.unobserve(r); } }); }
  revealNear(); addEventListener('load', revealNear);

  // rail + progress
  var panels = [].slice.call(document.querySelectorAll('.panel'));
  var railItems = [].slice.call(document.querySelectorAll('.rail-item'));
  var order = ['hero','history','map','llm','tokens','embeddings','attention','rag','agents','dial','context','archetypes','cloud','reliability','energy','beyond','frontier','questions'];
  var progBar = document.getElementById('progBar');
  function railFor(r){ return railItems.find(function(b){ return b.getAttribute('data-goto')===r; }); }
  var ao = new IntersectionObserver(function (entries) {
    entries.forEach(function (e) {
      if (!e.isIntersecting) return;
      var rung = e.target.getAttribute('data-rung'); var idx = order.indexOf(rung);
      railItems.forEach(function (b) {
        b.classList.remove('active');
        var bi = order.indexOf(b.getAttribute('data-goto'));
        if (bi > -1 && bi < idx) b.classList.add('done'); else b.classList.remove('done');
      });
      var cur = railFor(rung); if (cur) cur.classList.add('active');
    });
  }, { threshold: 0.5 });
  panels.forEach(function (p) { ao.observe(p); });
  function updProg(){ var h=document.documentElement; var m=h.scrollHeight-h.clientHeight; progBar.style.width=(m>0?(h.scrollTop/m*100):0).toFixed(1)+'%'; }
  addEventListener('scroll', updProg, { passive: true }); updProg();

  // nav
  function gotoRung(r){ var el=document.getElementById(r); if(el) el.scrollIntoView({behavior: reduce?'auto':'smooth', block:'start'}); }
  document.querySelectorAll('[data-goto]').forEach(function(b){ b.addEventListener('click', function(){ gotoRung(b.getAttribute('data-goto')); }); });
  function curIdx(){ var mid=scrollY+innerHeight*.5; for(var i=panels.length-1;i>=0;i--){ if(panels[i].offsetTop<=mid) return i; } return 0; }
  addEventListener('keydown', function(e){
    if(e.target.tagName==='INPUT'||e.target.tagName==='TEXTAREA') return;
    var i=curIdx();
    if(['ArrowDown','ArrowRight','PageDown'].indexOf(e.key)>-1){ e.preventDefault(); if(i<panels.length-1) panels[i+1].scrollIntoView({behavior:reduce?'auto':'smooth'}); }
    else if(['ArrowUp','ArrowLeft','PageUp'].indexOf(e.key)>-1){ e.preventDefault(); if(i>0) panels[i-1].scrollIntoView({behavior:reduce?'auto':'smooth'}); }
  });

  // fire an animation once when its stage enters view
  function onEnter(sel, fn){
    var el = document.querySelector(sel); if(!el) return;
    var done=false;
    var o=new IntersectionObserver(function(es){ es.forEach(function(e){ if(e.isIntersecting && !done){ done=true; fn(el); o.unobserve(e.target); } }); }, {threshold:.35});
    o.observe(el);
  }

  // ---- 02 next-token predict ----
  onEnter('[data-anim="predict"]', function(){
    var slot=document.getElementById('predictSlot'), bars=document.getElementById('predictBars');
    var opts=[['build things',0.42],['write code',0.27],['start',0.16],['read docs',0.09],['give up',0.06]];
    bars.innerHTML='';
    opts.forEach(function(o){
      var row=document.createElement('div'); row.style.cssText='display:flex;align-items:center;gap:10px;font-family:var(--mono);font-size:12px';
      row.innerHTML='<span style="width:92px;color:var(--text-dim)">'+o[0]+'</span>'+
        '<span style="flex:1;height:9px;background:var(--surface-2);border-radius:5px;overflow:hidden"><i style="display:block;height:100%;width:0;background:linear-gradient(90deg,var(--ocean),var(--ocean-soft));transition:width 1s ease '+(reduce?0:0.2)+'s"></i></span>'+
        '<span style="width:38px;text-align:right;color:var(--amber)">'+Math.round(o[1]*100)+'%</span>';
      bars.appendChild(row);
      requestAnimationFrame(function(){ row.querySelector('i').style.width=(o[1]*100)+'%'; });
    });
    setTimeout(function(){ slot.textContent='build things'; }, reduce?0:1100);
  });

  // ---- 03 tokens ----
  var tokSets=[
    [['Embed',''],['dings',''],[' turn','sp'],[' words','sp'],[' into','sp'],[' vectors','sp'],['.','']],
    [['un',''],['believ',''],['ably','']],
    [['G',''],['PT',''],['-',''],['4',''],[' writes','sp'],[' Python','sp'],['.','']]
  ];
  var tokLine=document.getElementById('tokLine'), tokCount=document.getElementById('tokCount'), tokId=100, tokTimer=null;
  function renderTokens(idx){
    if(tokTimer){ clearInterval(tokTimer); tokTimer=null; }
    tokLine.innerHTML=''; var set=tokSets[idx]; var chips=[];
    set.forEach(function(t){
      var c=document.createElement('span'); c.className='tok-chip';
      var txt=t[0].replace(/^ /,''); var lead=t[1]==='sp'?'<span class="sp">␣</span>':'';
      c.innerHTML=lead+txt.replace(/</g,'&lt;')+'<span class="tid">#'+(tokId++)+'</span>';
      tokLine.appendChild(c); chips.push(c);
    });
    tokCount.innerHTML='';
    var n=0;
    tokTimer=setInterval(function(){
      if(n>=chips.length){ clearInterval(tokTimer); tokTimer=null; tokCount.innerHTML='<b>'+chips.length+' tokens</b> · ≈ 4 characters each · this is what gets counted &amp; priced'; return; }
      chips[n].classList.add('in'); n++;
    }, reduce?1:130);
  }
  document.querySelectorAll('[data-tok]').forEach(function(b){ b.addEventListener('click', function(){ tokId=100; renderTokens(+b.getAttribute('data-tok')); }); });
  onEnter('[data-anim="tokens"]', function(){ renderTokens(0); });

  // ---- 04 embeddings ----
  var embWords=[
    ['dog','animal',.24,.30],['cat','animal',.32,.23],['wolf','animal',.17,.39],['horse','animal',.30,.41],
    ['king','royal',.75,.24],['queen','royal',.83,.31],['prince','royal',.71,.36],['throne','royal',.88,.21],
    ['bread','food',.20,.74],['apple','food',.30,.82],['coffee','food',.15,.65],['pizza','food',.32,.70],
    ['python','tech',.75,.72],['server','tech',.85,.66],['function','tech',.70,.79],['agent','tech',.83,.82]
  ];
  onEnter('[data-anim="embeddings"]', function(stage){
    var svg=document.getElementById('embSvg'); var dots=[];
    embWords.forEach(function(w){
      var d=document.createElement('button'); d.className='emb-dot g-'+w[1]; d.textContent=w[0];
      d.setAttribute('data-x',w[2]); d.setAttribute('data-y',w[3]); stage.appendChild(d);
      dots.push({el:d,x:w[2],y:w[3],name:w[0]});
    });
    requestAnimationFrame(function(){ requestAnimationFrame(function(){ dots.forEach(function(o){ o.el.style.left=(o.x*100)+'%'; o.el.style.top=(o.y*100)+'%'; o.el.classList.add('placed'); }); }); });
    function clearLines(){ svg.innerHTML=''; dots.forEach(function(o){ o.el.classList.remove('hot','dim'); }); }
    function neighbors(o){ return dots.filter(function(x){return x!==o;}).map(function(x){return {o:x,d:Math.hypot(x.x-o.x,x.y-o.y)};}).sort(function(a,b){return a.d-b.d;}).slice(0,3); }
    function drawFor(o){
      clearLines(); var r=stage.getBoundingClientRect(); o.el.classList.add('hot');
      var nb=neighbors(o); var keep={}; keep[o.name]=1; nb.forEach(function(n){ keep[n.o.name]=1; });
      dots.forEach(function(x){ if(!keep[x.name]) x.el.classList.add('dim'); });
      nb.forEach(function(n){
        n.o.el.classList.add('hot');
        var l=document.createElementNS('http://www.w3.org/2000/svg','line');
        l.setAttribute('x1',o.x*r.width); l.setAttribute('y1',o.y*r.height);
        l.setAttribute('x2',n.o.x*r.width); l.setAttribute('y2',n.o.y*r.height);
        l.setAttribute('stroke','var(--coral)'); l.setAttribute('stroke-width','1.4'); l.setAttribute('stroke-dasharray','4 4'); l.setAttribute('opacity','.65');
        svg.appendChild(l);
      });
    }
    dots.forEach(function(o){ o.el.addEventListener('mouseenter', function(){ drawFor(o); }); o.el.addEventListener('focus', function(){ drawFor(o); }); o.el.addEventListener('click', function(){ drawFor(o); }); });
    stage.addEventListener('mouseleave', clearLines);
    // drop query
    var qdot=null;
    document.getElementById('embDrop').addEventListener('click', function(){
      if(qdot) return;
      var q={x:.26,y:.33,name:'puppy'};
      var d=document.createElement('button'); d.className='emb-dot g-query'; d.textContent='puppy ◆';
      stage.appendChild(d); qdot={el:d,x:q.x,y:q.y,name:q.name}; dots.push(qdot);
      requestAnimationFrame(function(){ requestAnimationFrame(function(){ d.style.left=(q.x*100)+'%'; d.style.top=(q.y*100)+'%'; d.classList.add('placed'); }); });
      d.addEventListener('mouseenter', function(){ drawFor(qdot); });
      d.addEventListener('click', function(){ drawFor(qdot); });
      setTimeout(function(){ drawFor(qdot); }, reduce?0:1150);
    });
    document.getElementById('embReset').addEventListener('click', function(){ if(qdot){ qdot.el.remove(); dots=dots.filter(function(x){return x!==qdot;}); qdot=null; } clearLines(); });
  });

  // ---- 05 attention ----
  var attTokens='The animal did n\'t cross the street because it was too tired .'.split(' ');
  var attW={
    'it': {'animal':1,'street':.45,'tired':.6,'cross':.25,'because':.2},
    'tired': {'it':.7,'animal':.55,'too':.5,'was':.3},
    'cross': {'animal':.7,'street':.9,'the':.3},
    'animal': {'it':.6,'cross':.4,'tired':.35}
  };
  onEnter('[data-anim="attention"]', function(){
    var wrap=document.getElementById('attSentence'), hint=document.getElementById('attHint'); wrap.innerHTML='';
    var spans={};
    attTokens.forEach(function(t){
      var s=document.createElement('span'); s.className='att-word'; s.textContent=t.replace('n\'t',"n't"); s.setAttribute('data-w',t);
      wrap.appendChild(s); spans[t]=s;
    });
    function focus(word){
      Object.keys(spans).forEach(function(k){ spans[k].classList.remove('focus','lit'); spans[k].style.background=''; spans[k].style.color=''; });
      var w=attW[word]; if(!w){ return; }
      spans[word].classList.add('focus');
      Object.keys(w).forEach(function(tgt){ if(spans[tgt]){ var a=w[tgt]; spans[tgt].classList.add('lit'); spans[tgt].style.background='rgba(74,167,236,'+(0.10+a*0.5)+')'; spans[tgt].style.color='#fff'; } });
      hint.innerHTML='"<b style="color:#fff">'+word+'</b>" attends most to → <b style="color:var(--ocean-soft)">'+Object.keys(w).sort(function(a,b){return w[b]-w[a];})[0]+'</b>';
    }
    Object.keys(spans).forEach(function(k){ spans[k].addEventListener('click', function(){ if(attW[k]) focus(k); }); });
    var cyc=['it','cross','tired']; var ci=0;
    setTimeout(function(){ focus('it'); }, reduce?0:400);
    if(!reduce){ setInterval(function(){ ci=(ci+1)%cyc.length; focus(cyc[ci]); }, 2600); }
  });

  // ---- 11 energy bars ----
  onEnter('[data-anim="energy"]', function(){
    var box=document.getElementById('energyBars');
    var rows=[
      ['Human brain','~20 watts',0.02,'var(--green)'],
      ['A gaming GPU','~300 watts',0.16,'var(--amber)'],
      ['One AI server (8 GPUs)','~10,000 watts',0.55,'var(--coral)'],
      ['Data center serving a model','megawatts +',1.0,'var(--coral)']
    ];
    box.innerHTML='';
    rows.forEach(function(r){
      var pct=parseFloat(r[2]);
      var row=document.createElement('div'); row.style.cssText='display:flex;align-items:center;gap:12px;font-family:var(--mono);font-size:12.5px';
      row.innerHTML='<span style="width:170px;color:var(--text-dim)">'+r[0]+'</span>'+
        '<span style="flex:1;height:12px;background:var(--surface-2);border-radius:6px;overflow:hidden"><i style="display:block;height:100%;width:0;background:'+r[3]+';transition:width 1.1s cubic-bezier(.2,.8,.3,1) .15s;border-radius:6px"></i></span>'+
        '<span style="width:120px;text-align:right;color:'+r[3]+'">'+r[1]+'</span>';
      box.appendChild(row);
      requestAnimationFrame(function(){ row.querySelector('i').style.width=Math.max(3,pct*100)+'%'; });
    });
  });

  // ---- 07 agent loop ----
  var steps=[].slice.call(document.querySelectorAll('#loopSteps .lstep'));
  var sStep=document.getElementById('sStep'),sCalls=document.getElementById('sCalls'),sMem=document.getElementById('sMem'),sStatus=document.getElementById('sStatus');
  var titles=['Observe','Reason','Act: retrieve','Observe result','Decide: loop','Act: file'];
  var mem=['denial','+ criteria goal','+ query sent','+ §220.2','+ appeal drafted','+ filed ✓'];
  var cur=-1, playing=null;
  function render(){
    steps.forEach(function(s,i){ s.classList.remove('on','past'); if(i===cur)s.classList.add('on'); else if(i<cur)s.classList.add('past'); });
    if(cur<0){ sStep.textContent='idle'; sCalls.textContent='0'; sMem.textContent='empty'; sStatus.textContent='waiting'; sStatus.style.color='var(--text-mute)'; }
    else { sStep.textContent=(cur+1)+' · '+titles[cur]; var c=0; if(cur>=2)c++; if(cur>=5)c++; sCalls.textContent=String(c); sMem.textContent=mem[cur];
      if(cur>=steps.length-1){ sStatus.textContent='done ✓'; sStatus.style.color='var(--green)'; } else { sStatus.textContent='running'; sStatus.style.color='var(--ocean-soft)'; } }
  }
  function step(){ if(cur>=steps.length-1) return false; cur++; render(); if(!reduce&&cur>=0) steps[cur].scrollIntoView({block:'nearest'}); return true; }
  function play(){ if(playing){ clearInterval(playing); playing=null; document.getElementById('loopPlay').textContent='▶ Run the loop'; return; }
    if(cur>=steps.length-1){ cur=-1; render(); } document.getElementById('loopPlay').textContent='❚❚ Pause';
    playing=setInterval(function(){ if(!step()){ clearInterval(playing); playing=null; document.getElementById('loopPlay').textContent='↻ Run again'; } }, reduce?1:900); }
  document.getElementById('loopPlay').addEventListener('click',play);
  document.getElementById('loopStep').addEventListener('click',function(){ if(playing){clearInterval(playing);playing=null;document.getElementById('loopPlay').textContent='▶ Run the loop';} if(!step()){cur=-1;render();} });
  document.getElementById('loopReset').addEventListener('click',function(){ if(playing){clearInterval(playing);playing=null;} document.getElementById('loopPlay').textContent='▶ Run the loop'; cur=-1; render(); });
  render();
})();
</script>

